  "language": "en",
  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "A fiber optic cable carries internet data as flashes of light inside a strand of glass. Instead of sending electrical pulses through copper, it guides light from one end to the other. At the cable's transmitter, a laser or LED turns digital data into rapid pulses of light. Those pulses enter the cable's narrow core, the central path reserved for the light. The core is wrapped in cladding, which lets light reflect at the boundary instead of escaping. Each pulse follows the fiber by repeated total internal reflection even when the cable bends. At the destination, a photo detector senses the arriving pattern of light and darkness. The receiver converts that pattern back into electrical data for your computer or router. So fiber optic internet moves information by guiding coded light through glass, then decoding it at the far end.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9627003005572727,
  "low_confidence_words": [],
